<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syria Palaestina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 135–c. 390</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> Palaestina was the Roman province formed when the emperor Hadrian renamed the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> after crushing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt in 135 CE. The new name, taken from the old Greek term for the coastal land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a>, removed Judaea from the map, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were barred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, rebuilt as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-capitolina">Aelia Capitolina</a>. Around 390 the province was divided into several Palaestina provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syria-palaestina.jpg" alt="Syria Palaestina" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomisti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Syria_Palaestina_(AD_210).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
